wireguard-go's fix of its IPv6 send address, proposed upstream in https://github.com/WireGuard/wireguard-go/pull/135
(open since 2025-12-20, not merged as of 2026-11-20), applied to the pinned commit b3bc704 by the lab's Dockerfile.

StdNetBind.Send takes the destination from a pooled net.UDPAddr and copied the address into its IP before resizing
it: once an IPv4 send had cut the pooled IP to 4 bytes, an IPv6 send copied only 4 of its 16 bytes and kept the
rest from before, so the datagram went to another address. In W7's lab (part 3a) the go peer then answered an IPv6
initiation at fd5e:3a91:: instead of fd5e:3a91:c2d4:77::1, once it had sent to IPv4 clients: 4 of 6 runs.

--- a/conn/bind_std.go
+++ b/conn/bind_std.go
@@ -367,12 +367,12 @@
 	defer s.udpAddrPool.Put(ua)
 	if is6 {
 		as16 := endpoint.DstIP().As16()
-		copy(ua.IP, as16[:])
 		ua.IP = ua.IP[:16]
+		copy(ua.IP, as16[:])
 	} else {
 		as4 := endpoint.DstIP().As4()
-		copy(ua.IP, as4[:])
 		ua.IP = ua.IP[:4]
+		copy(ua.IP, as4[:])
 	}
 	ua.Port = int(endpoint.(*StdNetEndpoint).Port())
